{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791057888,"nonce":"8RXFPG8PjKaUcJ1fOi04vP1y7NQ=","title":"Glyph atlas never reclaimed space, so a cold glyph held its slot until clear()","message":"GlyphAtlas grew to its 4096 cap and never shrank. Nothing distinguished\na glyph the current frame draws from one last used a thousand frames\nago, so the only way to reclaim was clear(), which drops everything.\n\nThat became worth fixing with subpixel phases, which multiply atlas\nentries per glyph by the phase count (measured at 2.81x for three\nphases). A GC turns that into a working-set multiple instead of a\npermanent one.\n\n\nEpoch marking: begin_epoch() once per frame, insert and lookup record\nthe epoch, and touch_glyph lets a caller with its own glyph cache report\na hit the atlas never saw. gc(max_age) reclaims from there.\n\nIt reclaims a shelf ONLY when every glyph on it has gone stale. That is\nthe constraint that makes it safe rather than a convenience: atlas pixel\ncoordinates are baked into already-emitted GPU primitives, and the\nrenderer caches GlyphInfo copies. Compacting live glyphs would reclaim\nmore and invalidate every one of them, needing a full text rebuild and\na batch invalidation. Resetting a wholly-stale shelf moves nothing\nlive, so no emitted primitive becomes wrong.\n\nReclaimed pixels are zeroed and marked dirty, so stale coverage cannot\nbleed into a glyph packed there later and the GPU stops sampling what\nthe CPU has freed.\n\nTextRenderer::gc_atlas clears the renderer's own glyph cache when\nanything was dropped, since those are GlyphInfo copies that would\notherwise point into reclaimed space.\n\noccupancy() is the figure a GC moves. utilization() measures how far\ndown the shelves reach and so never falls, which makes it useless for\nwatching reclamation.\n\n\nFreshness was , so a glyph inserted at epoch 0 and\ngc(0) made the cutoff equal its own epoch and it read as already\nexpired: the GC would evict what the next frame was about to draw. It is\n now, and a missing last_used reads as stale rather than defaulting\nto fresh.\n\n\nNothing in Blinc calls begin_atlas_epoch or gc_atlas. The API is there\nand tested, but installing an eviction policy in the live render loop\nchanges runtime behaviour and wants a rendered frame to verify, which\nthis work has not had. Two one-line calls in the frame loop when that\nverification happens.","files":null}]}